I think literacy is at the heart

278
00:10:38,510 --> 00:10:40,143
of a lot of these conversations.

279
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:43,800
This question is for Alex.

280
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:45,600
As a student leader

281
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:47,400
and a student leader in peer support,

282
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:49,200
how do you see your peers relationships

283
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:50,550
with AI and mental health?

284
00:10:50,550 --> 00:10:52,230
Is AI adding to their stress

285
00:10:52,230 --> 00:10:53,550
or are students beginning

286
00:10:53,550 --> 00:10:56,100
to use it as a tool for support?

287
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:59,190
- Yeah, so as you might expect, it's both

288
00:10:59,190 --> 00:11:00,960
on the stressor side,

289
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:03,210
we're obviously seeing concerns over the job market,

290
00:11:03,210 --> 00:11:05,340
which was already not too great for us.

291
00:11:05,340 --> 00:11:06,810
I just graduated.

292
00:11:06,810 --> 00:11:07,800
I am, you know,

293
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:08,633
working as a nanny.

294
00:11:08,633 --> 00:11:09,810
So that says that.

295
00:11:09,810 --> 00:11:13,170
But I think that for a lot of fields outside of, you know,

296
00:11:13,170 --> 00:11:15,000
myself, I have friends in,

297
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,200
you know, computer science

298
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:18,840
or business and there's a lot of concern that a lot

299
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:20,040
of these entry level

300
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:22,080
job positions are gonna be replaced

301
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:24,000
by AI and we are already seeing it

302
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:25,470
and we're seeing that a lot

303
00:11:25,470 --> 00:11:27,720
of these companies now want

304
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:29,280
their employees to be able

305
00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:32,647
to use AI and have AI literacy to make sure that they,

306
00:11:32,647 --> 00:11:35,040
you know, can be the most efficient workers

307
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:36,810
and that it wouldn't make more sense

308
00:11:36,810 --> 00:11:39,300
to just replace them completely with AI.

309
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:40,890
So that's definitely a big area

310
00:11:40,890 --> 00:11:43,710
that I'm seeing my peers struggling with.

311
00:11:43,710 --> 00:11:46,950
And then also in terms of school, there's a lot

312
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:50,280
of talk obviously about using AI for classwork

313
00:11:50,280 --> 00:11:52,500
and so it, there's an ethical struggle there

314
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:54,540
because you know, sometimes it's, yeah,

315
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:56,520
it's great for bouncing ideas off of

316
00:11:56,520 --> 00:11:58,950
and it's great for you know, organizing stuff,

317
00:11:58,950 --> 00:12:00,450
but then there's a question

318
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:03,720
of when does it become the AI doing your work for you

319
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:04,800
and how is that impacting

320
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:06,120
your critical thinking skills?

321
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:08,790
And you know, we're seeing that, you know,

322
00:12:08,790 --> 00:12:10,830
some job fields are feeling

323
00:12:10,830 --> 00:12:12,600
that the entry level individuals

324
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:13,440
they are getting aren't

325
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:14,790
as prepared as in the past.

326
00:12:14,790 --> 00:12:18,870
And a reason for this is possibly because of AI use

327
00:12:18,870 --> 00:12:20,640
and not having the same level

328
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:22,500
of critical thinking skills.

329
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:23,520
So there's that.

330
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,100
And then in terms of AI literacy,

331
00:12:26,100 --> 00:12:27,720
people would think that Gen Z, you know,

332
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:28,980
has the best skills.

333
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:31,530
They know how to use AI, they know how to avoid

334
00:12:31,530 --> 00:12:33,990
wrong answers, but in reality we're seeing

335
00:12:33,990 --> 00:12:38,340
that even though Gen Z is quite dependent on it,

336
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:39,450
they're not able to catch

337
00:12:39,450 --> 00:12:40,530
when it's doing something wrong

338
00:12:40,530 --> 00:12:41,970
or giving a bad answer.

339
00:12:41,970 --> 00:12:44,043
So that's definitely detrimental as well.

340
00:12:45,450 --> 00:12:47,190
But on the positive side of things,

341
00:12:47,190 --> 00:12:48,450
as kind of discussed AI

342
00:12:48,450 --> 00:12:50,400
for mental health support has been really great.

343
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:52,200
I have a lot of friends who have, yeah,

344
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:54,780
as kind of discussed use ChatGPT

345
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:57,150
as their personal therapist as a friend.

346
00:12:57,150 --> 00:12:59,880
And we're seeing that this is a great kind of way

347
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:02,670
for like entry level mental health help for people

348
00:13:02,670 --> 00:13:04,350
who either can't afford therapy

349
00:13:04,350 --> 00:13:07,140
or are a little hesitant to it.

350
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:09,960
So it's a low, you know, entry point

351
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:11,700
for mental health assistance,

352
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:13,170
which I think can definitely spur

353
00:13:13,170 --> 00:13:14,899
a lot of change in terms

354
00:13:14,899 --> 00:13:17,340
of pushing individuals

355
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:20,670
to seek out formal therapy eventually as well.

356
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:21,960
And you know, there's no stigma,

357
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:24,260
they're not worried about judgment, et cetera.

358
00:13:25,710 --> 00:13:29,220
And yeah, I think that's kind of the two main things.

359
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:30,180
Yeah.

360
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:31,200
- That was really excellent,

361
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:32,640
Alex, I also really like that barrier

362
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:36,330
to entry point you made as being a hurdle to therapy

363
00:13:36,330 --> 00:13:37,920
and how there is potential,

364
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:38,880
there is already potential

365
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:41,220
that AI is like a way to jump that hurdle.

366
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:42,053
Excellent.

367
00:13:43,110 --> 00:13:45,840
This is for Gil, career anxiety is one

368
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:47,850
of Gen Z's biggest stressors, especially

369
00:13:47,850 --> 00:13:50,340
with AI reshaping the future of work.

370
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:52,500
How is this showing up among students

371
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:54,900
and what can colleges do to help them navigate

372
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:56,760
that uncertainty?

373
00:13:56,760 --> 00:13:59,550
- Yeah, so I'm definitely seeing career anxiety

374
00:13:59,550 --> 00:14:01,740
as a stressor of our students, especially

375
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:04,350
with AI reshaping the future of our work.

376
00:14:04,350 --> 00:14:07,170
I think there's a lot of uncertainty about

377
00:14:07,170 --> 00:14:10,320
how AI will affect career choices

378
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:12,120
and decisions that our students make.

379
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:16,110
And I think what I'm seeing is that it is

380
00:14:16,110 --> 00:14:17,820
influencing them in a way where they're trying

381
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:19,470
to see if there's a way to avoid it.

382
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:22,350
So they're choosing more hands-on careers,

383
00:14:22,350 --> 00:14:24,393
healthcare, trade programs.

384
00:14:25,710 --> 00:14:28,020
And what I'm also learning

385
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:29,520
through our future summit today

386
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:31,380
is that our, you know,

387
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:35,250
a lot of our entire workforce is being reshaped by AI.

388
00:14:35,250 --> 00:14:37,950
So it's not something that they can necessarily avoid.

389
00:14:37,950 --> 00:14:40,860
So these are things that if we're,

390
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:43,380
it's great if it's their intrinsic reason,

391
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:45,480
but if their decision making

392
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:47,430
is based on a perceived threat

393
00:14:47,430 --> 00:14:48,690
of AI, that's definitely

394
00:14:48,690 --> 00:14:51,570
worth exploring with our students.

395
00:14:51,570 --> 00:14:53,580
I think also students already

396
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:55,710
outside of AI may struggle

397
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:59,850
with their sense of worth just as an individual.

398
00:14:59,850 --> 00:15:00,683
And so the systems

399
00:15:00,683 --> 00:15:02,340
that our students exist

400
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:04,380
in can make students feel like

401
00:15:04,380 --> 00:15:05,280
they're not enough

402
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:06,960
or that they're not doing enough.

403
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,810
And so it's not uncommon for people

404
00:15:09,810 --> 00:15:12,390
to tie their self-worth to their work.

405
00:15:12,390 --> 00:15:13,320
Okay.

406
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,510
And so if they're hearing

407
00:15:15,510 --> 00:15:19,260
that AI is taking over positions, there's this idea

408
00:15:19,260 --> 00:15:22,650
that AI has more value than people

409
00:15:22,650 --> 00:15:26,010
and that can create self-doubt, self-criticism,

410
00:15:26,010 --> 00:15:29,220
it can worsen anxieties, leaving people

411
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:31,353
to really question their own value,

412
00:15:32,340 --> 00:15:35,550
what's left for them if AI can already

413
00:15:35,550 --> 00:15:37,740
do parts of their their job.

414
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:41,250
And so career anxiety is not just about worries

415
00:15:41,250 --> 00:15:43,860
for stability and a good paying job,

416
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:47,430
it's also about their identity and purpose.

417
00:15:47,430 --> 00:15:52,080
And so I think our California community colleges,

418
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:55,050
especially what I'm seeing throughout our summit,

419
00:15:55,050 --> 00:15:59,640
you know, we really are leading our AI revolution,

420
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:02,130
creating new career narratives.

421
00:16:02,130 --> 00:16:04,290
And so hearing what's happening in the workforce

422
00:16:04,290 --> 00:16:07,200
development, I do feel hopeful

423
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:10,890
hearing our students are the ones building the future.

424
00:16:10,890 --> 00:16:12,930
I also am hearing professors

425
00:16:12,930 --> 00:16:15,000
and how they're integrating AI literacy

426
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,680
into their curriculum.

427
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:20,250
And so there are ways we can learn to use AI ethically

428
00:16:20,250 --> 00:16:22,410
and work alongside it.

429
00:16:22,410 --> 00:16:23,610
I heard great examples

430
00:16:23,610 --> 00:16:26,700
of how professors are working with AI

431
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:30,600
by having students really show how they use AI ethically

432
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:32,850
and responsibly and not avoid it.

433
00:16:32,850 --> 00:16:36,120
And so there are things like internships, career fairs,

434
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:37,500
workshops, guest speakers.

435
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:40,020
I think there's more opportunities we can utilize

436
00:16:40,020 --> 00:16:42,900
to bring industry experts to discuss

437
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:44,760
how AI is shifting roles

438
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:46,530
and what skills are most needed,

439
00:16:46,530 --> 00:16:49,380
what job options there are in the field,

440
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:51,270
and how we can still continue

441
00:16:51,270 --> 00:16:54,120
to combine human creativity, critical thinking

442
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:57,000
with the efficiency of AI.

443
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,420
I think like, again, you're gonna hear this

444
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:01,980
more than once for me, but like AI

445
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:04,050
or not, I think our students,

446
00:17:04,050 --> 00:17:05,760
because they're still learning, there's a lot

447
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:07,950
that they don't know about career fields

448
00:17:07,950 --> 00:17:09,540
'cause they're not in them yet.

449
00:17:09,540 --> 00:17:12,570
There's a lot that they don't know about it, right?

450
00:17:12,570 --> 00:17:13,800
There's a lot of what they hear.

451
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:18,210
And so I do think it's important for them to, you know,

452
00:17:18,210 --> 00:17:20,970
hear from the experts so that they can,

453
00:17:20,970 --> 00:17:22,080
which can also support

454
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:25,470
some of that career anxiety around

455
00:17:25,470 --> 00:17:29,430
how AI is reshaping the fields,

456
00:17:29,430 --> 00:17:30,600
'cause again, they're,

457
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:33,540
the anxieties could promote them

458
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:37,710
to want to run away, (laughs), versus really address

459
00:17:37,710 --> 00:17:38,970
what skills they need to know

460
00:17:38,970 --> 00:17:43,860
to really compliment human work with AI.

461
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:46,800
I also think our educators need to continue to,

462
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:48,510
you know, model a growth mindset

463
00:17:48,510 --> 00:17:51,330
by continuing our own professional development

464
00:17:51,330 --> 00:17:54,690
and AI so that we are keeping current

465
00:17:54,690 --> 00:17:58,230
with emerging technologies similar to making time

466
00:17:58,230 --> 00:18:02,130
to this last couple days for our summit.

467
00:18:02,130 --> 00:18:03,870
I'll quickly just give an example.

468
00:18:03,870 --> 00:18:05,280
Like when COVID first hit,

469
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:06,420
there was a lot of fears

470
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:09,690
of going virtual for like our mental health services

471
00:18:09,690 --> 00:18:10,890
in the CC system.

472
00:18:10,890 --> 00:18:13,800
People were like there's no way we can do telehealth.

473
00:18:13,800 --> 00:18:16,350
Like it was really only seen

474
00:18:16,350 --> 00:18:18,870
as a model of in-person care

475
00:18:18,870 --> 00:18:21,450
and we learned ethical telehealth use

476
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:24,099
and that really transformed our mental health model

477
00:18:24,099 --> 00:18:27,540
and now it's a service we don't go without, right?

478
00:18:27,540 --> 00:18:30,000
Like it's increased access for our students.

479
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,520
And I think this is something

480
00:18:32,520 --> 00:18:35,250
we can also adapt to regarding AI.

481
00:18:35,250 --> 00:18:39,030
And so I think our Gen Z students,

482
00:18:39,030 --> 00:18:39,863
they have the right

483
00:18:39,863 --> 00:18:42,990
to feel excited and nervous,

484
00:18:42,990 --> 00:18:44,400
but I think we want them

485
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:47,700
to be able to see that there's room

486
00:18:47,700 --> 00:18:51,070
for both and all, right, so they can

487
00:18:52,260 --> 00:18:55,020
make room for their identity, their purpose

488
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:58,293
and technology can coexist with it.

489
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:00,600
- Fantastic Gil, thank you.

490
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:02,430
I also really like the perspective,

491
00:19:02,430 --> 00:19:04,951
I'm hearing this a lot from the panelists,

492
00:19:04,951 --> 00:19:06,000
like this access barrier,

493
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,380
like this barrier to entry.

494
00:19:07,380 --> 00:19:09,630
If AI can remove that in terms

495
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:11,640
of getting mental health help,

496
00:19:11,640 --> 00:19:13,050
I think that's incredible.

497
00:19:13,050 --> 00:19:14,970
Whether, you know, we may have our doubts about it,

498
00:19:14,970 --> 00:19:18,120
et cetera, but removing barriers to entry is what we,

499
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:20,790
especially I feel like community colleges do best.

500
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:22,470
So that's very interesting.

501
00:19:22,470 --> 00:19:24,750
This next question is for Xuan,

502
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:27,690
Flourish is a purpose built research validated AI app

503
00:19:27,690 --> 00:19:29,040
for student mental health.

504
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:31,230
Why AI and what opportunity

505
00:19:31,230 --> 00:19:32,790
do you see AI play in promoting

506
00:19:32,790 --> 00:19:34,770
student mental health?

507
00:19:34,770 --> 00:19:35,603
- Awesome.

508
00:19:35,603 --> 00:19:37,170
I'll continue on access.

509
00:19:37,170 --> 00:19:40,260
So, well, one question is access for who, right?

510
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:41,400
Because in the past when we think

511
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:43,350
about therapy counseling,

512
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:45,450
usually students who are already struggling

513
00:19:45,450 --> 00:19:47,100
and they may have been quietly

514
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:48,600
sitting with the challenges

515
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:51,120
for months and finally decide to reach out for help.

516
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:53,610
And we know how long students sit

517
00:19:53,610 --> 00:19:55,560
with the challenges without asking for help.

518
00:19:55,560 --> 00:19:57,630
So what's really cool about AI

519
00:19:57,630 --> 00:19:58,920
and this is how we think

520
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:00,000
about mental health,

521
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,120
is that mental health is a continuum.

522
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,820
Like maybe 15% of people are in the red zone.

523
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:09,540
They are deeply in a concerning state,

524
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:11,520
maybe clinically depressed

525
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:15,180
or anxious, they need professional support.

526
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:17,760
But then there are, let's say 80%

527
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:19,020
of people in the yellow zone

528
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:21,660
or orange zone, maybe having

529
00:20:21,660 --> 00:20:24,570
their fair share of rejections

530
00:20:24,570 --> 00:20:27,690
or stress moments when they feel demotivated

531
00:20:27,690 --> 00:20:31,860
or stressed out, they may have conflict

532
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:35,250
with their peers or their parents

533
00:20:35,250 --> 00:20:37,050
or their colleagues.

534
00:20:37,050 --> 00:20:39,510
And then they may also have

535
00:20:39,510 --> 00:20:42,840
at night have racing thoughts, can't fall into sleep.

536
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:45,930
So they are not in the clinical side of things.

537
00:20:45,930 --> 00:20:48,660
They may not seek out for mental health support.

538
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:51,000
Also because it can be expensive,

539
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:52,650
the waiting time can be long.

540
00:20:52,650 --> 00:20:55,410
But with AI it can actually guide people

541
00:20:55,410 --> 00:20:57,180
to learn coping strategies

542
00:20:57,180 --> 00:20:59,040
and to make better sense

543
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,030
of what they are going through

544
00:21:00,030 --> 00:21:01,920
and take actions to improve it,

545
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:03,720
to move towards the green zone.

546
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:06,450
So that's how we started thinking about AI.

547
00:21:06,450 --> 00:21:09,720
So we founded Flourish with

548
00:21:09,720 --> 00:21:11,320
another colleague from Stanford,

549
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:15,060
Department of Psychology.

550
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:16,410
And the way we think about

551
00:21:16,410 --> 00:21:18,690
AI is not as AI therapist,

552
00:21:18,690 --> 00:21:21,123
I think that's the wrong way to think about AI.

